bounded critical number, and those with density slightly less than 1/4 can have arbitrarily large critical number.\nTHE CRITICAL NUMBER OF DENSE  \nTRIANGLE-FREE BINARY MATROIDS\n\nJIM GEELEN AND PETER NELSON\n\n**Abstract.** We show that, for each real number ε > 0 there is an integer c such that, if M is a simple triangle-free binary matroid with |M| ≥ (1/4 + ε) 2^{r(M)}, then M has critical number at most c. We also give a construction showing that no such result holds when replacing 1/4 + ε with 1/4 − ε in this statement. This shows that the “critical threshold” for the triangle is 1/4. We extend the notion of critical threshold to every simple binary matroid N and conjecture that, if N has critical number c ≥ 3, then N has critical threshold 1 − i · 2^{-c} for some i ∈ {2, 3, 4}. We give some support for the conjecture by establishing lower bounds.\n\n1. INTRODUCTION\n\nIf M is a simple binary matroid, viewed as a restriction of a rank-r projective geometry G ≅ PG(r − 1, 2), then the critical number of M, denoted χ(M), is the minimum nonnegative integer c such that G has a rank-(r − c) flat disjoint from E(M). A matroid with no U_{2,3}-restriction is triangle-free. Our first two main theorems are the following:\n\n**Theorem 1.1.** For each ε > 0 there exists c ∈ ℤ such that every simple triangle-free binary matroid M with |M| ≥ (1/4 + ε) 2^{r(M)} satisfies χ(M) ≤ c.\n\n**Theorem 1.2.** For each ε > 0 and each integer c ≥ 1, there is a simple triangle-free binary matroid M such that |M| ≥ (1/4 − ε) 2^{r(M)} and M has critical number c.\n\nThat is, simple triangle-free binary matroids with density slightly more than 1/4 have bounded critical number, and those with density slightly less than 1/4 can have arbitrarily large critical number. Theorem 1.2 refutes an earlier conjecture of the authors [13]. As in [13], the\n\nDate: May 12, 2015.  \n1991 Mathematics Subject Classification. 05B35.  \nKey words and phrases. matroids, regularity.  \nThis research was partially supported by a grant from the Office of Naval Research [N00014-10-1-0851].\n\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nExact threshold for non-linear Hamilton cycles - Harvard Math (https://www.math.harvard.edu/event/exact-threshold-for-non-linear-hamilton-cycles/)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.9 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; # Exact threshold for non-linear Hamilton cycles ... Speaker: Byron Chin (MIT) ... The result confirms behavior conjectured by Narayanan and Schacht.\n\n# Exact threshold for non-linear Hamilton cycles\n\nHARVARD-MIT COMBINATORICS\n\nWhen: December 5, 2024\n\n4:00 pm - 5:00 pm\n\nWhere: MIT, Room 2-139\n\nSpeaker: Byron Chin (MIT)\n\nIn this talk, I will discuss some recent work determining the threshold for the appearance of Hamilton cycles — that is a cycle using all of the vertices — in random hypergraphs. The result confirms behavior conjectured by Narayanan and Schacht. The main difficulty comes from the fact that the usual second moment method does not suffice. I will talk about a technique to improve the second moment method, known as small subgraph conditioning, and how it applies to this setting.\n\nFor information about the Richard P. Stanley Seminar in Combinatorics, visit https://math.mit.edu/combin/--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe critical number of dense triangle-free binary matroids (https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jctb.2015.08.003)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; # The critical number of dense triangle-free binary matroids☆\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Jim Geelen, Peter Nelson ... We extend the notion of critical threshold to every simple binary matroid N and conjecture that, if N has critical number , then N has critical threshold for some .\n\n# The critical number of dense triangle-free binary matroids☆\n\nAuthor links open overlay panelJim Geelen, Peter Nelson\n\nShow more\n\nView 