-----------------\n1116-05-1563 (https://jointmathematicsmeetings.org/amsmtgs/2181_abstracts/1116-05-1563.pdf)\nciteturn1search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Dense binary PG(t −1, 2)-free matroids have critical ... We resolve two conjectures of Geelen and Nelson, showing that the critical threshold of PG(t −1, 2) is 1 −3 · 2−t.\n1116-05-1563\nJonathan Tidor* (jtidor@mit.edu). Dense binary PG(t −1, 2)-free matroids have critical\nnumber t −1 or t.\nThe critical threshold of a (simple binary) matroid N is the inﬁmum over all ρ such that any N-free matroid M with\n|M| > ρ2r(M) has bounded critical number. In this talk, we study the critical threshold of the projective geometry\nPG(t −1, 2) as a generalization of a classical problem in graph theory. No knowledge of matroid theory will be assumed.\nWe resolve two conjectures of Geelen and Nelson, showing that the critical threshold of PG(t −1, 2) is 1 −3 · 2−t. We\ndo so by proving the following statement: if M is PG(t −1, 2)-free with |M| > (1 −3 · 2−t)2r(M), then the critical number\nof M is t −1 or t. (Received September 20, 2015)\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSharp thresholds for nonlinear Hamiltonian cycles in hypergraphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.05142)\nciteturn1academia16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.4 years ago; Authors: Bhargav Narayanan, Mathias Schacht ... Such cycles are said to be linear when $\\ell = 1$, and nonlinear when $\\ell > 1$. We determine the sharp threshold for nonlinear Hamiltonian cycles and show that for all $r > \\ell > 1$, the threshold $p^*_{r, \\ell} (n)$ for the appearance of a Hamiltonian $\\ell$-cycle in the random $r$-uniform hypergraph on $n$ vertices is sharp and is $p^*_{r, \\ell} (n) = \\lambda(r,\\ell) (\\frac{\\mathrm{e}}{n})^{r - \\ell}$ for an explicitly specified function $\\lambda$.\nTitle: Sharp thresholds for nonlinear Hamiltonian cycles in hypergraphs\nAuthors: Bhargav Narayanan, Mathias Schacht\nDate: Wed Jun 12 13:58:57 2019\n\nFor positive integers $r > \\ell$, an $r$-uniform hypergraph is called an $\\ell$-cycle if there exists a cyclic ordering of its vertices such that each of its edges consists of $r$ consecutive vertices, and such that every pair of consecutive edges (in the natural ordering of the edges) intersect in precisely $\\ell$ vertices. Such cycles are said to be linear when $\\ell = 1$, and nonlinear when $\\ell > 1$. We determine the sharp threshold for nonlinear Hamiltonian cycles and show that for all $r > \\ell > 1$, the threshold $p^*_{r, \\ell} (n)$ for the appearance of a Hamiltonian $\\ell$-cycle in the random $r$-uniform hypergraph on $n$ vertices is sharp and is $p^*_{r, \\ell} (n) = \\lambda(r,\\ell) (\\frac{\\mathrm{e}}{n})^{r - \\ell}$ for an explicitly specified function $\\lambda$. This resolves several questions raised by Dudek and Frieze in 2011.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nExact threshold and limiting distribution for non-linear Hamilton cycles | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/ko/abs/2411.13452)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.8 years ago; Crawled: last month; As a result we pin down the exact threshold for the appearance of non-linear Hamilton cycles in random hypergraphs, confirming a conjecture of Narayanan and Schacht. ... @misc{chin2025exactthresholdlimitingdistribution, title={Exact threshold and limiting distribution for non-linear Hamilton cycles}, author={Byron Chin}, year={2025}, eprint={2411.13452}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.13452}, }\nSubmitted 06 Jan 2025\n\n# Exact threshold and limiting distribution for non-linear Hamilton cycles\n\nImageMIT\n\nBC\n\nByron Chin\n\n## Abstract\n\nFor positive integers $r > \\ell \\geq 1$r>ℓ≥1, an $\\ell$ℓ-cycle in an $r$r-uniform hypergraph is a cycle where each edge consists of $r$r vertices and each pair of consecutive edges intersect in $\\ell$ℓ vertices. For $\\ell \\geq 2$ℓ≥2, we determine the limiting distribution of the number of Hamilton $\\ell$ℓ-cycles in an Erdős--Rényi random hypergraph. The behavior is distinguished in two cases: -When $\\ell \\geq 3$ℓ≥3, the number of cycles concentrates when the expectation diverges and converges to a Poisson distribution when the expectation is constant. -When $\\ell = 2$ℓ=2, the normalized number of cycles converges to a lognormal distribution when the expectation diverges and converges to a lognormal mixture of Poisson distributions when the expectation is constant. As a result we pin down the exact threshold for the appearance of non-linear Hamilton cycles in random hypergraphs, confirming a conjecture of Narayanan and Schacht.\n\nView PaperPDF\n\n1\n\nCite\n\n## BibTeX\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{chin2025exactthresholdlimitingdistribution, title={Exact threshold and limiting distribution for non-linear Hamilton cycles}, author={Byron Chin}, year={2025}, eprint={2411.13452}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.13452}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDense binary $PG(t-1,2)$-free matroids have critical number $t-1$ or $t$ (https://arxiv.org/abs/1508.07278)\nciteturn1academia17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.2 years ago; Title: Dense binary $PG(t-1,2)$-free matroids have critical number $t-1$ or $t$ ... In this paper, we resolve two conjectures of Geelen and Nelson, showing that the critical threshold of the projective geometry $PG(t-1,2)$ is $1-3\\cdot2^{-t}$.\nTitle: Dense binary $PG(t-1,2)$-free matroids have critical number $t-1$ or $t$\nAuthors: Jonathan Tidor\nDate: Fri Aug 28 16:59:36 2015\n\nThe critical threshold of a (simple binary) matroid $N$ is the infimum over all $\\rho$ such that any $N$-free matroid $M$ with $|M|>\\rho2^{r(M)}$ has bounded critical number. In this paper, we resolve two conjectures of Geelen and Nelson, showing that the critical threshold of the projective geometry $PG(t-1,2)$ is $1-3\\cdot2^{-t}$. We do so by proving the following stronger statement: if $M$ is $PG(t-1,2)$-free with $|M|>(1-3\\cdot2^{-t})2^{r(M)}$, then the critical number of $M$ is $t-1$ or $t$. Together with earlier results of Geelen and Nelson [GN14] and Govaerts and Storme [GS06], this completes the classification of dense $PG(t-1,2)$-free matroids.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBHARGAV NARAYANAN AND MATHIAS SCHACHT (https://www.math.uni-hamburg.de/home/schacht/2020/nonlin-thresh.pdf)\nciteturn1search18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.4 years ago; BHARGAV NARAYANAN AND MATHIAS SCHACHT ... namely that of when a random \\(r\\)-graph contains a Hamiltonian cycle; our main contribution is to resolve the sharp threshold problem for nonlinear Hamiltonian cycles. ... A moment’s thought should convince the reader that, unlike in the case of linear Hamiltonian cycles where one has to worry about isolated vertices, there are no ‘coupon collector type’ obstacles to the presence of nonlinear Hamiltonian cycles; therefore, the conclusion of Theorem 1.1 should not come as a surprise.\nFinally, by convention, an \\(\\ell\\)-cycle is called linear (or loose) when \\(\\ell=1\\), nonlinear when \\(\\ell>1\\), and tight when \\(\\ell=r-1\\).\n\nGiven \\(r>\\ell\\ge 1\\), we set\n\n\\[\n\\lambda(r,\\ell)=t!\\cdot (s-t)!,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(s=r-\\ell\\) and \\(1\\le t\\le s\\) is the unique integer satisfying \\(t=r \\pmod{s}\\), and define\n\n\\[\np^*_{r,\\ell}(n)=\\frac{\\lambda(r,\\ell)^e s}{n^s}.\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(G^{(r)}(n,p)\\) for the binomial random \\(r\\)-graph on \\(n\\) vertices, where each possible \\(r\\)-set of vertices appears as an edge independently with probability \\(p\\), our main result is as follows.\n\nTheorem 1.1. For all integers \\(r>\\ell>1\\) and all \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), as \\(n\\to\\infty\\) with \\((r-\\ell)\\mid n\\), we have\n\n\\[\n\\mathbb{P}\\big(G^{(r)}(n,p)\\text{ contains a 